matroskadec: read the cues again at the next seek when their read failed on I/O

The cues of most files lie after the clusters, and matroskadec reads them
at the first seek (cues_parsing_deferred). When that read fails, it marks
the index broken (cues_parsing_deferred = -1) and the cues read
(elem->parsed = 1) for good: every later seek of the input knows only the
clusters played so far and reads on from the last of them, cluster by
cluster, to its target. On a network input whose connection dropped during
the first seek (a player's resume seek right after the open), that read
can be the whole film: once the network was back, a resume to 600 s of a
5 GB file read and decoded from 4 s for a minute.

A seek to the cues or a read of them that failed on I/O (the seek failed,
or the read left an error on the AVIOContext: a network error, an
interrupt), at their start or within them, now leaves them unread: what
was read is dropped and they are read again at the next seek. An error
that is not a network one (a 4xx) is given up on after three such reads,
as before. Invalid data and a clean end (a truncated file whose cues lie
past its end) still mark the index broken, so a local file reads on from
its clusters as before. An error an earlier read left on the context is
cleared for the seek, so it does not make a clean end look like I/O.

A seek that fails on I/O now fails with its error: the read of the cues,
the read of the clusters on to the target, or the repositioning to the
cluster it lands on (whose failure was ignored: the seek reported success
and the next read met the error, or the end of the file, there). The
generic search avformat falls back to fails with it too (its first read,
from where the search starts, returns it), where it would read the file
from the last known cluster. A seek to a target within the clusters
already read still lands.

Our own patch (upstream FFmpeg master has the same code); upstreamable.
Checked by tests/matroska (mkv-cues-*, mkv-loaded-*).

